WEBVTT

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.780
Waiting for their orders.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:33.890
Commanding officers speaking.

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:36.530
Stand by for second labor drop.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:02:00.000
Zero-one to Comanche.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:09.190
"Enemy labor located. Coordinates 5301. Range 1.2 kilometers."

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:45.590
Officer to Unit 1, continue your advance south-west for attack when visual contact established.

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:47.220
All units, stand by.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:30.000
sees the completion of the foundations of Kisarar's

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:35.730
island together with its sister man-made island kawasaki 2 kisarazu is central to tokyo's

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:41.090
development plan the distance between the two islands is 15 kilometers and the bridge that

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:46.310
will eventually span the gap between them will require an outstanding feat of engineering but

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:50.870
on completion tokyo will possess a system of sluice gates that will maintain the efficiency

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:55.870
of its drainage system by using the force of the ties even more important will be the

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:58.900
contribution to the solution of the land shortage problem

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:04.950
As we enter the 21st century, no less than 45,000 hectares of newly created land will

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:12.150
result from this unique engineering masterpiece, a truly historic achievement.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:16.270
Operation Babylon, the plan of the city fathers, is that Tokyo become an example to compare

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:20.930
with that pearl of the Old Testament, the Babylon of modern-day Japan.

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:24.430
This vast construction project, the greatest of the century, has not been completed without

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:25.270
the odd hiccup.

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:30.000
However, the wide-ranging use of labor has enabled these to be overcome.

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:33.980
underwater construction. Extremely complex technologically would not have

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:38.560
been feasible without the extensive use of labors. And even above ground, or should

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:42.880
I say above water, labors have been productively and cost-effectively employed

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:47.000
in bringing the project to its present stage. However, the development of

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:50.620
specialized labors, which has seen advances over the whole field of robotics,

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:55.380
has produced some surprising, not always welcome results. For example, the term

00:06:55.380 --> 00:07:00.000
labor sabotage has entered our language and examples of rogue laborers which have

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:04.150
sabotage human creative effort though thankfully still rare have nevertheless

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:08.240
become too frequent to be ignored specialist scientists are devoting an

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:12.020
ever-increasing proportion of time and resource to combating this potential

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:15.850
danger we're lucky to have with us this morning two of the brightest stars in

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:21.880
this field huh you are two of the brightest stars in the field aren't you

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:28.920
and as I speak we're approaching the ark and what an imposing sight it is 500

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:30.000
meters long and rise

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.360
150 meters above the waterline.

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:34.150
A large part of its massive

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:36.010
interior is devoted to labor design,

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:37.490
manufacture, and maintenance.

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:40.640
Do you have any idea why they named it the Ark?

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:41.920
How would I know?

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:44.640
The number of labors that have passed through the Ark

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:46.580
is no less than 3,600.

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:48.860
Okay, I think we've solved the problem

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:49.880
in System 05.

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:52.160
The display's working perfectly now.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:55.110
Thank God for that. Sound okay, too?

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:56.770
Sound okay. No problems.

00:07:57.220 --> 00:07:59.230
Well, no problems we can't cure, anyhow.

00:07:59.230 --> 00:08:00.000
The engine

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.620
The engineers at Shinohara Industries are pretty good and killed most of the bugs at the plant.

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:08.000
By the time we double-check their checks, every last circuit will be guaranteed over 100%.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:11.950
Your pal Shige may be a software genius, but some of us know our jobs as well.

00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:17.710
So you can tell him to calm down, and I promise him the new labors will be delivered on time with all the technical falls under control.

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:21.130
You guys never make mistakes, do you? I'm gonna reboot the backup. Stand by.

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:25.900
Wait, Shinobu. Two members of the second section have just landed and are on their way to see you.

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:28.130
Division 10? Why?

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:34.710
- Boy, what a sight!

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:36.570
Wasn't it worth it, making the trip here

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:38.890
to see the best labor in the world?

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:40.490
- Oh, well I suppose so,

00:08:40.490 --> 00:08:42.270
but couldn't they have made it look friendlier?

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:43.400
- What?

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:45.700
I persuaded Captain Godot to give us the day off

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:47.000
to come here to see this labor,

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:48.080
and now you say you're not interested.

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:49.820
- I didn't say I wasn't interested.

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:51.640
- Okay, that's it for today.

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:52.280
- Roger.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:05.880
Azuma Shinohara and Noah Izumi reporting for a labor inspection, sir.

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:09.440
It isn't sir, it's ma'am. But you can call me Shinobu.

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:14.540
Thank you, sir. We were invited to inspect your latest labors and come out on the radio chopper.

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:17.460
The maintenance team's following us by speedboat to take us back.

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:22.190
Tell us, ma'am, will Type Zero be ready for delivery by the projected date?

00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:25.890
You know about Labor Zero, obviously. You would as part of Godot's team.

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:29.430
Do you want to inspect the Zero's pilot system?

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.360
Not that Miss Izumi sounds if she's particularly impressed.

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:34.020
She overheard what I said.

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:36.040
Well, you just had to open your big mouth as usual.

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:38.030
Don't think she goes in for the jokes much.

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:39.930
I'll have to be more careful.

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:42.610
It's true, though. It doesn't look friendly at all.

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:44.380
Another laborer'd think it does.

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:47.100
Why are they calling it the Type Zero?

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:49.360
Because it's both first and last.

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:51.200
The last of the 98 series.

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:55.480
But it's the first laborer to be specifically designed to incorporate the HOS.

00:09:55.950 --> 00:09:56.720
What's HOS?

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:00.000
You don't really know very much, do you, for a labor operator?

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:04.350
It's the software controlling labor action and movement efficiency.

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:05.210
It's BrainCore.

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:06.890
You mean it's OS.

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:09.690
It used to be that, but a guy at Shinohara Heavy Industries

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:11.890
has built in a new revolutionary procedure.

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:15.730
OS, the operating system in the old labors, is enlarged by over 30%,

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:17.730
and so their efficiency is increased by the same.

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:19.640
A hyper-operating system.

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:21.230
It's a brand new concept.

00:10:21.230 --> 00:10:24.210
Labors are becoming a new breed with the HO system.

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:26.710
Sounds pretty good, but even these Type 0 labors

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:28.090
are still going to depend on human intelligence and--

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:29.730
Intelligence and courage.

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:36.590
"Chief Shinobo Nagumo, Special Vehicle Division, your boat has arrived at Dock A-15 to take you to the mainland. Dock A-15..."

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:38.590
Hey, they sure didn't waste any time.

00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:40.610
Okay, let's go.

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:56.580
Will you look at the size of this?

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:59.160
Everything here is scaled to labor size.

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:02.700
Hey you guys, we don't have time to admire the view.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:03.700
Yes ma'am!

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:10.640
Electrical management team on level B. Check system Z-20A through F-81.

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:13.640
Shinobu ma'am, can I ask something that I find a bit puzzling?

00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:16.640
You better talk to someone on staff here who knows more than I do about the place.

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:20.530
I just wondered why they decided to bring Type Zero here to work on.

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:29.730
You know the New York Police Department took delivery of three Zero types some months ago on a trial basis and it seems they had certain teething troubles.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.920
Well what did they expect, with an idiot like Kanaka running the development team?

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:37.920
So Shige was sent to the states to supervise the investigation, and he reported that all

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:42.020
labors should be checked out thoroughly with operatives for any possible signs of operational

00:11:42.020 --> 00:11:43.020
faults.

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:46.720
As the Ark is owned by Shinohara Heavy Industries and our division of vehicle operatives is

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:48.920
based nearby, it seemed the ideal site.

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:51.180
What have you discovered so far?

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:52.130
Nothing.

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:54.950
Nothing has yet emerged that gives us cause for concern.

00:11:54.950 --> 00:11:59.030
But if Shige's worrying, it means there has to be a damn good reason, so we're checking.

00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:14.660
- You're extending for Section's training program?

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:17.060
I was given to understand my guys would be here next week

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:18.660
and then two weeks leave.

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:20.080
- That was the idea.

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:22.280
But unfortunately, I've had to change plans.

00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:24.660
As a result, Section One will continue training

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:27.530
on labor type zero for a further week minimum.

00:12:27.530 --> 00:12:29.830
- I think you owe me a word of explanation, sir.

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:31.560
And in any case, in my opinion,

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:34.540
second section isn't competent to protect the city by itself,

00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:36.760
considering the overtime that you're demanding of them.

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:40.760
I didn't like being separated from my section in the first place,

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:42.260
and I'm not having it extended.

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:45.640
I'm sorry. The chief has sent me this order personally.

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:48.170
I happen to know the reason why it has to be enforced.

00:12:48.490 --> 00:12:50.170
That's great. Well, I don't,

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:53.130
and I'm going straight to the chief to ask him to return 1st Division

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:55.390
to active patrol duty under my command again.

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:56.660
Stop right there.

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:00.000
I'm going to tell you,

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:04.240
something which is not to go beyond these four walls three days ago a

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:08.020
prototype labor of the 7th Division's Defense Force Battalion suddenly went on

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:11.860
the rampage during a normal scheduled wind tunnel test it broke out of the

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:15.590
laboratory and got off the base through the perimeter wire it took a combined

00:13:15.590 --> 00:13:18.770
army and top airborne labor force operation to finally stop it in its

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:22.560
tracks no one knows why it went berserk and by the time they shot it up there

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:25.800
was damn little left to investigate except useless bits of scrap what's

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:28.920
that got to do with section one's training program the labor was made by

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:30.000
Shinahara heavy industry

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:34.840
It was equipped with the same new operating system, the HOS, currently being installed

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:36.160
in the Zero-type.

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:39.960
And though the military tried to conceal the fact when the labor was immobilized, its cockpit

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:40.850
was empty.

00:13:40.850 --> 00:13:44.310
Are you beginning to see the implications now, Captain?

00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:48.750
It looks as if this new technology is leading us into unknown territory, where, so far at

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:51.050
least, it seems as if we're not totally in control.

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:52.510
Hey!

00:13:52.510 --> 00:13:54.480
What?

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:56.110
Give me a break.

00:13:56.110 --> 00:13:57.110
Oh, God.

00:13:57.110 --> 00:13:58.100
What sin have I committed?

00:13:58.100 --> 00:14:00.000
Why do I deserve this?

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:02.480
What kind of weak little wimps are you?

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:05.220
The reason you joined the force to begin with is to protect the place,

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:08.840
so that's exactly what we'll do till the captain has given us the green light and sends us on leave.

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:11.920
And who's going to protect my rights to an uninterrupted night's sleep?

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:13.360
The police don't have rights!

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:14.820
What a dumb thing to say.

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:18.000
I've only been home three times in two months,

00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:20.760
and that makes for certain conjugal problems, believe me.

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:23.160
Then you shouldn't have gotten married in the first place.

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:25.740
I suppose Oda's got a point, and we've just got to do it.

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:29.210
But you know there've been more calls than normal in the last weeks.

00:14:29.210 --> 00:14:29.670
Ah!

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:34.230
Second section, scramble, scramble, scramble.

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:36.930
A 205 challenge progress in sector 7.

00:14:37.170 --> 00:14:38.230
Section 2, scramble, scramble, scramble.

00:14:38.230 --> 00:14:39.270
Must have hurt us, I guess.

00:14:39.850 --> 00:14:40.390
Let's go.

00:14:40.730 --> 00:14:41.490
Come on, you guys.

00:14:41.970 --> 00:14:45.500
Get moving, wake up, let's have this show on the road fast.

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:48.330
Get those laborers on the street, move.

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:50.580
Move.

00:15:00.830 --> 00:15:02.390
evacuated in an order way.

00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:06.170
Hey, you played a sideshow or something?

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:07.620
Get that out of here!

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:23.820
Target labor is identified in Yasabushi Construction Company's Pirate 2000, cutting a path in

00:15:23.820 --> 00:15:28.160
a straight line southeast from International Street, moving at a speed of approximately

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:29.860
four kilometers per hour.

00:15:30.760 --> 00:15:35.580
Yonohara, keep on going along your present route toward the river and see if you can stop it at the bridge.

00:15:35.900 --> 00:15:36.480
Okay, Captain.

00:15:36.620 --> 00:15:39.300
Ota, pursue and destroy. Put that labor out of action.

00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:41.250
Count on it, that mother.

00:15:42.130 --> 00:15:45.470
Calm down, Ota. Remember that saving lives is our first priority.

00:15:45.710 --> 00:15:46.820
Ota, Ota.

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:49.540
Jinchi, when I need a lecture, I'll let you know.

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:52.320
Shouldn't you give him a straight order not to file?

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:54.060
I'm sure he'll act sensibly.

00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:54.840
Raise him!

00:16:16.030 --> 00:16:19.630
The Joker's a Yatsubishi Tyrant 2000, so be doubly careful, eh, Noah?

00:16:19.630 --> 00:16:21.280
It ain't got much speed.

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:22.880
That's a mean punch.

00:16:22.880 --> 00:16:25.990
I wish I could get out of assignments like this.

00:16:25.990 --> 00:16:28.130
Hey!

00:16:28.130 --> 00:16:29.920
Hmm?

00:17:00.380 --> 00:17:06.240
Okay, I was gonna go easy on you because you're a taxpayer, but I'm not taking insults!

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:08.240
Hold on, fat face!

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:16.800
And the White Knight from Section 2's back on his feet squaring up.

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:20.800
And there's a series of forearm jolts to the power pack that have Tyrant reeling back.

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:26.240
And the Tyrant sends White Knight flying with a kick to the groin.

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:29.670
But the White Knight's up again with a transverse stranglehold.

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:36.940
Do something about the pilot!

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:41.850
Goddamn thing just won't lie down!

00:17:41.850 --> 00:17:44.050
Get the pilot out!

00:17:45.700 --> 00:17:49.380
Otak, use the electric drive!

00:17:49.380 --> 00:17:53.360
Go for the ram unit left side of its chest!

00:17:53.360 --> 00:17:55.040
One hit should be enough!

00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:56.670
Eat this!

00:17:56.670 --> 00:17:59.510
That cuts you down to size!

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:01.000
Mission completed, Captain!

00:18:01.830 --> 00:18:03.450
What?!

00:18:03.450 --> 00:18:04.850
Now hold on!

00:18:04.850 --> 00:18:05.850
Don't get hasty!

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:19.060
Starting to get mad!

00:18:19.060 --> 00:18:25.260
Trailers 1 and 2 ready to make pickup.

00:18:48.520 --> 00:18:52.580
to heat check all circuits and every last program yes sir what happened that

00:18:52.580 --> 00:18:55.940
damn fool who piloted my labor in the drink they're defrosting him in the

00:18:55.940 --> 00:19:00.000
bath I'll warm him up when I see him sir I can't explain

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:07.090
Explain it. A Tyrant 2000 that's been completely reliable suddenly rejects pilot control and takes off on its own.

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:11.160
I can't explain it either, but that was the way that it happened. I saw it.

00:19:11.400 --> 00:19:14.760
And of course, Trigger Happy Oda wrecked it so badly that there's nothing left to investigate.

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:19.390
Maybe we're just overreacting. We've all become exhausted with all the missions we've done.

00:19:19.670 --> 00:19:23.030
Overreacting or not, we've got to find the reason. What did the pilot have to report?

00:19:23.350 --> 00:19:27.810
Nothing at all of any help. A routine project, and it moved off suddenly out of control.

00:19:27.810 --> 00:19:29.510
He would say exactly that.

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:30.000
if he

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:33.360
caused this morning's chaos and panic captain my doubt if it's as simple as

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:36.940
that up to two months ago we hadn't met this kind of trouble and suddenly we get

00:19:36.940 --> 00:19:42.560
22 cases I have to tell you the section starting to get a little nervous huh

00:19:42.560 --> 00:19:47.840
where will your report put the blame what I report isn't necessarily what the

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:51.060
guys at the top will be willing to release for public consumption they'll

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:54.040
go along with the manufacturer's assurance that a system's fault could

00:19:54.040 --> 00:19:57.660
never cause a modern labor to go on the rampage because government and big

00:19:57.660 --> 00:20:00.000
business have got too much at stake I'm willing

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:01.360
to bet you a month's pay.

00:20:01.360 --> 00:20:04.170
They'll put it down conveniently to pilot error.

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:06.210
They're not gonna threaten the whole program

00:20:06.210 --> 00:20:08.110
because a section of mobile cops

00:20:08.110 --> 00:20:13.450
is getting a little nervous.

00:20:13.450 --> 00:20:15.490
- Food.

00:20:15.490 --> 00:20:16.950
- Zuma, it's my last bag,

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:18.450
and I'm dying of starvation.

00:20:18.450 --> 00:20:19.990
- Well, when you finish stuffing your face,

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:21.790
I want you to get together all the mission briefing

00:20:21.790 --> 00:20:23.570
and debriefing reports for the last month.

00:20:23.570 --> 00:20:24.470
- Huh?

00:20:24.470 --> 00:20:25.610
Okay, but what do you want 'em for?

00:20:25.610 --> 00:20:27.720
- Chop, chop!

00:20:27.720 --> 00:20:28.800
- Hey! - So cocky!

00:20:30.190 --> 00:20:38.700
into the fridge you mean the mainframe yeah okay but log it thanks you really

00:20:38.700 --> 00:20:41.400
have learned how to make the guys in your section do what you want them to

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:44.910
you're pretty good at it yourself but let's stick with our labor problem

