00:42:05.220 --> 00:42:15.230
tell me why did you suddenly invite me out when you never did before oh i just wanted to oh tell

00:42:15.230 --> 00:42:24.490
me now what have you been having trouble with alphonse recently so that's why you asked me out

00:42:24.490 --> 00:42:29.440
why why do you ask i was wondering whether the new system's all right

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:31.380
Alphonse always runs perfectly.

00:42:31.940 --> 00:42:35.920
Sakaki and the other mechanics are always saying he's their favorite laborer in the division.

00:42:36.780 --> 00:42:38.600
Listen, Alphonse will do what I tell him.

00:42:39.610 --> 00:42:41.870
He won't run amok, I promise.

00:42:41.870 --> 00:42:44.690
He won't go on the rampage like the others.

00:43:00.090 --> 00:43:04.340
- So you were listening at the office.

00:43:04.340 --> 00:43:06.430
- Look, it'll all be okay.

00:43:06.430 --> 00:43:09.090
Alphonse won't go crazy, I guarantee it.

00:43:09.090 --> 00:43:10.270
- You can't, Noah.

00:43:10.270 --> 00:43:12.830
I'm the only person who could guarantee it is dead.

00:43:12.830 --> 00:43:15.690
I've been trying as hard as I can to come up with a solution,

00:43:15.690 --> 00:43:17.230
but everything I've tried on the computer

00:43:17.230 --> 00:43:18.570
has drawn a complete blank.

00:43:18.570 --> 00:43:21.050
- Why can't we reinstate the operating system he had?

00:43:21.050 --> 00:43:23.130
Then there'll be no danger anymore.

00:43:23.130 --> 00:43:24.790
- But the system can only be changed

00:43:24.790 --> 00:43:26.450
by engineers at the factory.

00:43:26.450 --> 00:43:29.190
They won't admit there's a fault with the Hyper-O system.

00:43:29.190 --> 00:43:30.000
At least not if we can't.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:34.360
get cast iron proof that that's really the reason the labor started malfunctioning yes but if we

00:43:34.360 --> 00:43:39.080
don't do something soon Alphonse might go on the rampage and I really love him then they'd have to

00:43:39.080 --> 00:43:43.440
destroy him and I don't want that to happen to him I know that if he did go mad they'd have to

00:43:43.440 --> 00:43:50.320
demolish him no give me time I'll do everything I can but I need time I don't want to see Alphonse

00:43:50.320 --> 00:44:00.000
destroyed even though you annoy me Azuma I think we make a good team Noah you just pile

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:01.680
Let Alphonse leave the thinking to me.

00:44:02.090 --> 00:44:04.910
Azuma, you're always so goddamn sure of yourself, it's sickening.

00:44:05.350 --> 00:44:07.450
Shut up and eat your pizza before it gets cold.

00:44:11.290 --> 00:44:17.560
Azuma, that big German shepherd looks just like Alphonse.

00:44:17.780 --> 00:44:18.000
Huh?

00:44:18.330 --> 00:44:22.290
Dear old Alphonse I, the dog they gave me on my eighth birthday.

00:44:22.780 --> 00:44:25.440
So your labor is really the second Alphonse in your life?

00:44:25.810 --> 00:44:27.370
Not quite. He's my third.

00:44:27.690 --> 00:44:29.670
The second Alphonse was a pet cat.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:32.150
Look at it, will ya? Wonder what's up with it?

00:44:32.150 --> 00:44:35.070
Huh? Maybe it can hear something.

00:44:35.070 --> 00:44:39.290
You know, dogs can hear all sorts of things that human ears can't pick up at all.

00:44:39.290 --> 00:44:43.210
Maybe the wind's making some sort of noise like that.

00:44:43.210 --> 00:44:45.710
That might be it. Wind!

00:44:45.710 --> 00:44:48.800
What's up? Why are you pulling a face? Is your pizza a bit too ethnic?

00:44:52.700 --> 00:44:58.230
Sound. Sounds human ears can't hear.

00:45:00.510 --> 00:45:04.110
No lie, I've got no time to explain, but I gotta check something out that can't wait.

00:45:04.110 --> 00:45:07.550
First you tell me I can't finish my dinner and now you're leaving me here?

00:45:07.550 --> 00:45:10.750
I want you to go straight to the captain as soon as you get back to base

00:45:10.750 --> 00:45:12.350
and tell him I'll be calling him later.

00:45:12.350 --> 00:45:13.250
See ya!

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:34.480
I tried to phone father before I came to you, but his secretary wouldn't put me through.

00:45:34.480 --> 00:45:38.130
My mother can't help me because he's never at home and she doesn't see him herself.

00:45:38.130 --> 00:45:40.440
Well, he's a very busy man.

00:45:40.440 --> 00:45:42.990
Sorry, the place is such a mess.

00:45:42.990 --> 00:45:45.420
My daughter's just brought her son for a visit.

00:45:45.420 --> 00:45:46.420
You mean you're a grandfather?

00:45:46.420 --> 00:45:50.890
You came on that toy bike, so no beer to drink, but I'll make you coffee, okay?

00:45:50.890 --> 00:45:52.470
No coffee, thanks.

00:45:52.470 --> 00:45:55.910
What I really want is for you to tell me the sound frequency that makes laborers go on

00:45:55.910 --> 00:45:56.820
the rampage.

00:46:03.200 --> 00:46:05.760
We've got the Sumitomo Tower in Shinjuku,

00:46:05.760 --> 00:46:08.560
the twin tower blocks in Parktown in the Bay Area.

00:46:08.560 --> 00:46:10.760
These towers make whistling sounds on the windows

00:46:10.760 --> 00:46:12.880
from the northwest at a particular speed.

00:46:12.880 --> 00:46:15.640
We know it happens even if human hearing can't pick it up.

00:46:15.640 --> 00:46:17.520
Then there's harp bridge that spans the water

00:46:17.520 --> 00:46:20.140
from Shibura to Daiba where things might be the same.

00:46:20.140 --> 00:46:22.220
A great high suspension bridge with cables

00:46:22.220 --> 00:46:23.720
acting like harp strings.

00:46:23.720 --> 00:46:25.490
Sounds that people can't hear,

00:46:25.490 --> 00:46:28.300
but a labor might pick them up with its sensor system.

00:46:28.300 --> 00:46:30.000
I think that sound might be the trigger

00:46:30.160 --> 00:46:31.480
which makes labors run amok.

00:46:31.890 --> 00:46:35.050
All these places use a large number of construction labors around them,

00:46:35.190 --> 00:46:38.590
and they're all within a rough circle where the recent rampages have been occurring.

00:46:39.130 --> 00:46:41.470
I think that Hobo used sound deliberately as a trigger.

00:46:41.900 --> 00:46:43.900
What he was doing this for isn't clear to me as yet.

00:46:44.280 --> 00:46:46.260
A genius like him could work anywhere he liked,

00:46:46.420 --> 00:46:49.580
but I think I understand why he decided to come to Shinohara Heavy Industries.

00:46:49.940 --> 00:46:52.500
He knew that if he could corner the market in new labors,

00:46:52.500 --> 00:46:57.070
then because of his greed, father would turn a blind eye to the way Hobo was planning to use them.

00:46:57.410 --> 00:47:00.000
I think Father's discovered the truth and that he can't fail.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:05.350
Face the world. The HOS system is now installed in nearly every labor in the country. If it's

00:47:05.350 --> 00:47:12.040
shown to be defective, the company will collapse. Father's panicked. That's why I can't reach

00:47:12.040 --> 00:47:20.460
him. Azuma, have you any idea what the consequences are for him? I can't help that. If it hadn't

00:47:20.460 --> 00:47:24.660
been me discovering the truth, someone would have stumbled onto it sometime or other. You've

00:47:24.660 --> 00:47:30.000
got to go public on this, no matter what unpleasant results it may have. You have to stop the

00:47:30.060 --> 00:47:34.490
destruction whatever it costs the company we can't let your grandson grow

00:47:34.490 --> 00:47:40.050
up thinking labors are evil

00:48:02.560 --> 00:48:06.800
solved it everything's gonna be fine it was worth it leaving you to walk home

00:48:06.800 --> 00:48:21.020
alone you're confined to your quarters for two weeks which is letting you off

00:48:21.020 --> 00:48:26.470
lightly I imagine you're aware of the rules you've broken I'm not major then

00:48:26.470 --> 00:48:30.000
I'll go through them absent from the base without leave interrogated

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:31.620
a civilian without authorization,

00:48:31.620 --> 00:48:34.120
using the divisional computer for private purposes,

00:48:34.120 --> 00:48:35.700
appropriating a motor scooter,

00:48:35.700 --> 00:48:37.920
riding set motor scooter without a helmet.

00:48:37.920 --> 00:48:40.060
You beginning to get the drift?

00:48:40.060 --> 00:48:42.800
Any one of these offenses would rate a severe punishment,

00:48:42.800 --> 00:48:44.260
and I really ought to be thinking

00:48:44.260 --> 00:48:46.870
about kicking you off the force altogether.

00:48:46.870 --> 00:48:49.410
Fortunately for you, Captain Gotoh has informed me

00:48:49.410 --> 00:48:51.550
of some mitigating circumstances.

00:48:51.550 --> 00:48:54.550
You should be grateful to him for covering your ass as usual.

00:48:54.550 --> 00:48:55.130
Dismissed.

00:48:55.130 --> 00:48:56.590
- Sir, permission to speak.

00:48:56.590 --> 00:48:58.520
I have proof the labor-- - Permission denied.

00:49:00.250 --> 00:49:03.490
Listen, you just don't appreciate what this is all about, do you?

00:49:03.530 --> 00:49:07.740
I have no obligation to explain my actions to you, but I'll say this much.

00:49:07.920 --> 00:49:10.580
Your father, the owner of Shinohara Heavy Industries,

00:49:10.660 --> 00:49:13.180
made a personal admission to the Minister of Trade

00:49:13.260 --> 00:49:15.880
that there's a serious fault in the hyper-operating system.

00:49:15.960 --> 00:49:19.860
The government called an emergency cabinet meeting and a quick decision was made.

00:49:20.130 --> 00:49:23.890
All labors will immediately be recalled for the hyper-O systems

00:49:23.970 --> 00:49:27.050
they now have installed to be replaced by the old O system.

00:49:27.450 --> 00:49:30.000
In an effort not to damage commercial interests,

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:34.900
The Cabinet has resolved to lighten the enormous cost of this refit with support from the Treasury.

00:49:34.900 --> 00:49:38.180
But you can't punish me while a real criminal goes free!

00:49:38.180 --> 00:49:42.300
They're gonna give Shinohara Industries the money to clean up the shit they've thrown all over Tokyo?

00:49:42.300 --> 00:49:43.420
That's not fair!

00:49:43.420 --> 00:49:44.240
Hey!

00:49:44.240 --> 00:49:48.790
Where'd you get off telling me that I'm not acting fair when you could be locked up in jail for what you've done?

00:49:48.790 --> 00:49:52.340
So you're gonna sit there and let him get away with a cover-up?

00:49:52.340 --> 00:49:55.280
Big business sticks together and to hell with the truth!

00:49:55.280 --> 00:49:58.550
I enjoy the force to put out of this shit!

00:50:02.490 --> 00:50:06.340
You know, Asuma, there's more ways than one to skin a cat.

00:50:06.340 --> 00:50:10.620
Flying off the handle and making wild accusations doesn't get results.

00:50:10.620 --> 00:50:13.920
How far do you think the government will go to protect Shinahara Industries,

00:50:13.920 --> 00:50:17.170
its main suppliers of labors for the Babylon Project?

00:50:17.170 --> 00:50:20.070
The government's got a big investment in the Babylon Project.

00:50:20.070 --> 00:50:22.570
If it goes down, the government will fall with it.

00:50:22.570 --> 00:50:25.870
So they'll make things as easy as they can for Shinahara Industries,

00:50:25.870 --> 00:50:29.140
and the destruction that's been caused will somehow be forgotten.

00:50:29.140 --> 00:50:30.000
Hey, there's no need to go.

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:34.070
for you to look depressed. You've done well. Pinning the trouble down to the hyper-system

00:50:34.070 --> 00:50:38.350
took a lot of brains. It's a fine piece of detective work. Though it's a pity it wasn't

00:50:38.350 --> 00:50:43.280
taken up. How did Fukushima know I was riding that motor scooter without wearing a helmet,

00:50:43.420 --> 00:50:49.310
eh? Oh, that was me. Noah told me and I passed the fact on to him. What? Well, thanks a lot!

00:50:50.090 --> 00:50:54.170
I figured you were exhausted. You couldn't go on with the investigation and be on the

00:50:54.170 --> 00:50:59.250
duty roster, too. So, Captain, does that mean we haven't given up on it? I retract what I said

00:50:59.250 --> 00:51:00.000
about you having

00:51:00.440 --> 00:51:05.620
I don't believe we can halt the rampages just by calling the labors in and putting back the operating system.

00:51:05.620 --> 00:51:11.760
You really think Hoba didn't anticipate a simple answer like that and killed himself without taking precautions to stop us?

00:51:11.760 --> 00:51:13.020
Doesn't seem likely.

00:51:13.020 --> 00:51:17.610
You always believed that Hoba planned this chaos every inch of the way.

00:51:17.610 --> 00:51:19.430
That's right. What do you think?

00:51:19.430 --> 00:51:22.700
It's scary, but I think so too.

00:51:22.700 --> 00:51:27.600
Asuma! He's back. Thought you'd want to know.

00:51:28.770 --> 00:51:30.000
Shige!

00:51:32.970 --> 00:51:34.590
- Is that shirt to rebel invaders?

00:51:34.590 --> 00:51:37.270
- Well, you aren't exactly wearing regulation uniform.

00:51:37.270 --> 00:51:39.470
- Ha, the major believes that I was naughty

00:51:39.470 --> 00:51:41.410
and gave me two weeks on suspension.

00:51:41.410 --> 00:51:44.150
- You go AWOL and break nearly every rule in the book.

00:51:44.150 --> 00:51:46.450
I think I would have done more than just suspend you

00:51:46.450 --> 00:51:48.090
if only for letting down the team.

00:51:48.090 --> 00:51:49.530
- That's pretty rich from you, Oda.

00:51:49.530 --> 00:51:51.870
When did you put the team before yourself?

00:51:51.870 --> 00:51:52.710
- Of yours!

00:51:52.710 --> 00:51:55.150
- Hey, settle down, take it easy, guys.

00:51:55.150 --> 00:51:57.700
You could show you're a little happier to see me back.

00:51:57.700 --> 00:52:00.000
So come on, children, no fighting, please.

00:52:00.190 --> 00:52:03.430
The first thing you've got to do is rebuild our labors installing the old system in them.

00:52:03.430 --> 00:52:04.430
She's right.

00:52:04.430 --> 00:52:06.530
Shige, it's vital that you make that your first job.

00:52:06.530 --> 00:52:07.530
The old system?

00:52:07.530 --> 00:52:08.530
Yes.

00:52:08.530 --> 00:52:09.530
In our '98s?

00:52:09.530 --> 00:52:10.530
The old OS.

00:52:10.530 --> 00:52:13.370
Hmm.

00:52:13.370 --> 00:52:14.370
Don't just sit there, Shige.

00:52:14.370 --> 00:52:17.270
We can't risk using our labors with the HOS installed.

00:52:17.270 --> 00:52:20.020
Getting it out may not be the answer, but we've got to try.

00:52:20.020 --> 00:52:22.580
I never installed the hypersystem in the first place.

00:52:22.580 --> 00:52:23.750
What?

00:52:23.750 --> 00:52:24.750
But HQ ordered you to.

00:52:24.750 --> 00:52:29.200
Yeah, but somehow I had a few doubts about this wonderful new hypersystem, so I re-equipped

00:52:29.200 --> 00:52:30.000
with dummy disks.

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:31.410
and dump the real ones.

00:52:31.410 --> 00:52:33.070
Didn't you notice your labor's handling

00:52:33.070 --> 00:52:35.780
was exactly the same after they'd been redesigned?

00:52:35.780 --> 00:52:36.510
No?

00:52:36.510 --> 00:52:39.070
The first morning I came here, I was told by Sakaki,

00:52:39.070 --> 00:52:41.750
"Never put some new fangled bits and pieces into a labor

00:52:41.750 --> 00:52:43.470
unless you're convinced they're an improvement."

00:52:43.470 --> 00:52:45.850
Well, I wasn't convinced your father's factory

00:52:45.850 --> 00:52:48.070
had been thorough enough with its testing program.

00:52:48.070 --> 00:52:50.410
Of course, if anybody had discovered what I'd done,

00:52:50.410 --> 00:52:51.590
they'd have fired me and lost

00:52:51.590 --> 00:52:53.530
the most brilliant software brain they had.

00:52:53.530 --> 00:52:56.310
But no one caught me, and I was right.

00:52:56.310 --> 00:53:00.000
But how come that nobody ever told you?

00:53:00.030 --> 00:53:05.080
Goto knew. He telephoned New York not all that long ago to hear what I had to suggest, and I told her.

00:53:05.080 --> 00:53:06.880
Captain Goto knew?

00:53:06.880 --> 00:53:08.400
Just what I'd expect.

00:53:08.400 --> 00:53:12.670
Kept it to himself so that Azuma would slave his guts out to find a solution.

00:53:12.670 --> 00:53:15.650
He used us like we're dummies he can pull strings on.

00:53:15.650 --> 00:53:18.000
Yeah, well, he sure pulled my strings.

00:53:18.000 --> 00:53:22.310
I thought I was his ally working for the same goal, but he just used me.

00:53:22.310 --> 00:53:24.690
Don't be too upset, Azuma.

00:53:24.690 --> 00:53:27.720
Goto, I'm gonna kill you!

00:53:42.730 --> 00:53:43.550
Ichi Hoba.

00:54:37.100 --> 00:54:38.160
Meichi Hobo.

00:54:41.210 --> 00:54:41.890
Look, Matsui.

00:54:43.160 --> 00:54:49.640
Yeah, look here.

00:54:49.940 --> 00:54:51.130
Can you decipher what it says?

00:54:51.830 --> 00:54:55.750
He bowed the heavens also and came down.

00:54:55.750 --> 00:55:00.000
And darkness was under his feet.

00:55:01.720 --> 00:55:05.530
It's an Old Testament text, but I've forgotten which book.

00:55:05.530 --> 00:55:09.190
Hoba may have taken it as the verse which inspired him to do what he did.

00:55:09.190 --> 00:55:13.360
I'm trying to figure in what way it's relevant to the chaos which he planned.

00:55:13.360 --> 00:55:17.340
Good question. I don't think I can help you. You'll have to work that out yourself.

00:55:17.340 --> 00:55:24.570
Hoba's trail stops here. It's the end of the road. It's a strange part of the city.

00:55:24.570 --> 00:55:30.000
About 20 years ago, they got permission to redevelop it and started demolition. Then the authorities changed

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:33.200
changed their minds and said nothing could be built here.

00:55:33.200 --> 00:55:38.120
So it was left derelict, in a sort of limbo.

00:55:38.120 --> 00:55:41.240
You know, the more I see of this city, the weirder it is.

00:55:41.240 --> 00:55:43.420
In the last weeks, I've seen parts of the place

00:55:43.420 --> 00:55:46.340
that seemed almost as if times had no effect on them,

00:55:46.340 --> 00:55:48.360
parts which were as they'd always been.

00:55:48.360 --> 00:55:49.980
And then, without warning,

00:55:49.980 --> 00:55:53.080
I'd be met by the site of a building 50 floors high.

00:55:53.080 --> 00:55:56.630
I'd swear that building wasn't there when I turned my back.

00:55:56.630 --> 00:55:58.350
I don't believe everything in the past

00:55:58.350 --> 00:55:59.830
is worth keeping forever.

00:56:01.280 --> 00:56:05.240
but it's happening so fast that we get confused and feel like helpless babies.

00:56:05.560 --> 00:56:10.380
This old beaten-up Quayside was once a wealthy suburb where guys came home in the evening,

00:56:10.700 --> 00:56:14.640
and now in a few years the bay in front of us will be filled and everything will be different.

00:56:15.180 --> 00:56:20.100
This view will be just a relic of a remote, distant past that no one remembers or cares about.

00:56:20.220 --> 00:56:23.390
I don't mind that, but I have my doubts it'll be an improvement.

00:56:24.350 --> 00:56:28.190
I wonder if that's the message that Hobo was trying to leave behind him.

00:56:32.040 --> 00:56:34.140
What is it mankind works toward?

00:56:34.140 --> 00:56:35.900
What is it mankind wants in the end?

00:56:35.900 --> 00:56:37.320
I've got no answers.

00:56:37.320 --> 00:56:40.040
An old man with a reputation for great wisdom

00:56:40.040 --> 00:56:41.500
wrote them down in the sand,

00:56:41.500 --> 00:56:46.780
but then the wind wiped them cruelly away.

00:56:46.780 --> 00:56:48.000
Not that way.

00:56:48.000 --> 00:56:50.180
Let it bite in its own good time.

00:56:50.180 --> 00:56:53.330
Well, I put you to a lot of trouble for nothing.

00:57:01.630 --> 00:57:04.950
You've never told me what you make of this business about Hoba.

00:57:05.360 --> 00:57:08.780
I would say he was a frightening man, evil and mysterious.

00:57:09.830 --> 00:57:15.090
But Hoba's dead, his body's deep in the bay, so let's leave him and leave his message there.

00:57:15.690 --> 00:57:18.450
Yes, well, let's hope he allows us to do that. Take care.

00:57:19.870 --> 00:57:25.250
They grow tomatoes. Off-duty crews grow tomatoes, and the division mechanics spend time fishing.

00:57:25.470 --> 00:57:29.260
You told me he's sharp. Sure, his blazes isn't what I'd say about him.

00:57:29.340 --> 00:57:30.000
That's your mistake.

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:40.770
mistake son he's the best hey you still think i'm reading too much into this what i'm not sure

00:57:40.770 --> 00:57:45.820
maybe but thank god you saw there were problems with the zero type labors early enough for us

00:57:45.820 --> 00:57:51.480
to insist on more tests i called kanuka in new york and asked her to check out hobo while he was

00:57:51.480 --> 00:57:57.920
at mit they nicknamed him a hova a hova which was what the old testament writers called god

00:57:57.920 --> 00:58:00.000
we agree hobo was a genius

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:07.350
But taking God's name is a bit much, but the name Jehovah is really mispronounced. It should be Yahveh or Yahaveh

00:58:07.350 --> 00:58:13.050
I don't know which I'm not an expert, but Jehovah is a mistake. That's become accepted in the course of time

00:58:14.430 --> 00:58:17.430
Apparently when they told Hoba that he went crazy with anger

00:58:17.430 --> 00:58:19.850
I'm beginning to know him

00:58:19.850 --> 00:58:25.770
I'm beginning to respect how he felt every day by his window looking out over Tokyo the schemes

00:58:25.770 --> 00:58:30.000
He dreamed up seeing skyscrapers take over so many parts of a harsh image

00:58:30.120 --> 00:58:30.880
personal city.

